#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Lane D Accept (plan §3 conventions, §3.1, invariant 10, §17.2).

    accept <WP> [--evidence FILE] [--root R]
        invariant10 -> pytest tests/accept/<wp>/ -> stub_scan over PATHS
        -> make laned-e2e (when the Makefile defines it) -> evidence
        With --evidence, pytest and e2e see LANED_EVIDENCE=<abs FILE>: the
        WP's own tests verify the evidence content (plan §3, §17.1 L4).
    accept <WP> --expect-fail [--root R]
        tests-only PRs for an unbuilt WP: tests collected and failing for a
        right reason only
    accept <WP> --verification --base REF --head REF [--root R]
        the run for MODE tests-only: MODE verification when it applies (rules
        below), else exactly --expect-fail
    accept <WP> --tests-correction --base REF --head REF [--root R]
        tests-only PRs for a WP already built on BASE (see MODE rules below)
    accept <WP> --check-pr --base REF --head REF [--pr-wps WP,WP...] [--correction] [--root R]
        rule 1 and the WP's PATHS over `git diff --name-only BASE...HEAD`;
        prints the MODE the PR job runs next (rules below); --pr-wps names
        every laned:<WP> label of the PR (BATCH and FEATURE rules below);
        --correction: the PR also carries laned:tests-correction;
        for a review WP RV<n>: MODE review iff the diff is exactly an add or
        modify of evidence/rv<n>.md (no tests run)
    accept <WP> --combined --base REF --head REF [--correction] [--root R]
        MODE combined (FEATURE rule): the WP's new/changed tests fail on
        BASE's code for a right reason, then the full Accept passes on HEAD
    accept <WP>[,<WP>...] --cross --base REF --head REF [--root R]
        cross-WP regressions (CROSS rule below); prints `CROSS <WP> PASS|KNOWN|FAIL`
    accept [<WP>] --freshness --base REF --head REF --main REF [--warn-only] [--root R]
        stale-base guard (FRESHNESS rule below); prints `FRESH ...` or
        `STALE BASE: update-branch and re-run` (exit 1); with --warn-only
        `WARNING STALE BASE ...` (exit 0)
    accept --merge-group --base REF --head REF --group-labels FILE [--root R]
        the merge queue's combined ref (MERGE-GROUP rule below); prints
        `GROUP-OWN <WPs>`, `GROUP <WP> <mode> PASS|FAIL (#N)` and `GROUP PASS|FAIL`
    accept selftest
        offline check of the review, tests-correction, verification,
        evidence, cross, freshness and merge-group modes
    accept --gate <STAGE> [--root R]
        evidence/gates/<stage>.json: right stage, every check passed

MODE rules of --check-pr (the PR job runs the named mode next):
    MODE implementation   the diff has no tests/accept/<wp>/ path of this WP
                          and stays inside the labelled WPs' PATHS -> full
                          Accept
    MODE combined         (rev6.17, FEATURE rule) the diff changes this WP's
                          tests/accept/<wp>/ (PATHS included) together with
                          code -> --combined: every NEW test (a test function
                          absent on BASE) of the WP, taken from HEAD, runs
                          against BASE's code (a temporary BASE worktree whose
                          tests/accept/<wp>/ is HEAD's) and must fail there
                          for a right reason (the expect-fail classifier); a
                          new test passing on BASE proves nothing and fails
                          the PR, unless the WP is a declared verification WP
                          (scripts/laned/verification_wps at BASE). A CHANGED
                          test (AST differs, or a helper / conftest / fixture
                          in its closure changed) must not fail for a wrong
                          reason there; at least one new or changed test must
                          fail for a right reason (unless verification WP or
                          laned:tests-correction). Then the full Accept
                          (invariant10, pytest, stub_scan over HEAD's PATHS,
                          e2e) must pass on HEAD. Ledger mode `combined` with
                          `tests_tree` and the PR number.
    MODE tests-only       the diff touches only tests/accept/<wp>/ and BASE has
                          no implementation of the WP (no L2 commit on BASE's
                          first-parent line: a commit after the one adding
                          PATHS that changes non-test files, all inside PATHS)
                          -> --verification (below), which is --expect-fail
                          over the WP's whole test dir unless the PR is a
                          verification PR
    MODE verification     printed by --verification, not by --check-pr: a
                          tests-only PR for a VERIFICATION WP, one whose row
                          only asserts properties of code another WP already
                          built. It applies iff (0) the WP is listed in
                          scripts/laned/verification_wps at BASE (RV5: the
                          PR cannot declare it; an unlisted WP always runs
                          --expect-fail), (a) BASE has no L2 commit for
                          the WP, (b) every non-test entry of the WP's PATHS
                          already exists on BASE (an exact file; some file
                          under a dir/ entry; some file matching a glob) and
                          (c) the full Accept (invariant10, pytest, stub_scan
                          over PATHS, e2e) passes on the PR's tests over BASE's
                          code. Then it prints `MODE verification` and passes;
                          the ledger records mode `verification` with
                          `tests_tree` (tree sha of tests/accept/<wp>/ at HEAD).
                          A PATHS entry missing on BASE (or a PATHS file with no
                          non-test entry) never gives verification; any failing
                          Accept step falls back to --expect-fail semantics
                          (at least one right-reason failure), recorded as
                          mode `expect-fail`, so a normal tests-only PR still
                          works. On merge the status board treats the merge
                          commit (the first first-parent commit changing only
                          tests/accept/<wp>/ whose test dir has that
                          tests_tree) as both L1 and L2 for the WP; the
                          push-to-main Accept run then gives L3 as usual.
    MODE tests-correction the diff touches only tests/accept/<wp>/ and BASE
                          already has the WP's implementation (L2 commit found)
                          -> --tests-correction: every test file the PR does
                          NOT change must pass against BASE's implementation;
                          the test files it changes run in expect-fail
                          classification: each failure must be for a right
                          reason (it is pending a follow-up implementation
                          PR), and all of them passing is fine. Any
                          wrong-reason failure, or any failure in an
                          unchanged test file, fails the PR.
                          A test file counts as changed when the PR adds or
                          edits it, or any file in its static closure inside
                          tests/accept/<wp>/: modules it imports (relative,
                          or absolute resolving into the WP dir; transitive),
                          ancestor conftest.py / __init__.py in the WP dir,
                          and non-.py files under the WP dir named by a string
                          constant in that closure (basename, WP-dir-relative
                          path, or a containing directory such as "fixtures").
    MODE evidence         the diff only adds or modifies evidence files named
                          by `--evidence <file>` in the WP's §3.0 Accept cell
                          (plan at BASE); check-pr prints `EVIDENCE <file>`
                          per file -> `accept <WP> --evidence <file>` for each,
                          recorded in the ledger as mode `evidence` with the
                          file's blob sha (the push to main then re-runs it as
                          push/accept --evidence, which is what L4 reads).
                          A declared evidence file together with any other
                          path, a deletion, or an evidence/-only diff naming a
                          file the Accept cell does not declare (and PATHS do
                          not cover) -> FAIL evidence.
    MODE review           RV<n> label, the diff is exactly evidence/rv<n>.md

BATCH rule (plan rev6.2 item (h); rev6.17): a tests-only PR may carry several
WPs' tests. When --pr-wps names more than one label and the diff is only under
tests/accept/, check-pr requires: every path is inside some
tests/accept/<wp>/ dir; each such dir belongs to a label of the set; each
label's dir is in the diff; every label is a WP (no RV<n>). Since rev6.17
there is no size cap and built and unbuilt WPs may mix: check-pr prints
`BATCH <WPs>`, `WPMODE <WP> tests-only|tests-correction` per WP and `MODE`
for the WP it was asked about (tests-correction when built on BASE, else
tests-only). Refusals: `FAIL rule1: batch-<reason>: ...` with reason
non-wp-label, duplicate-label, stray-tests, unlabelled-tests or
label-without-tests. The PR job runs each WP's --verification (its own
TESTS-TREE in the ledger) or --tests-correction separately and fails when any
WP fails. A single label keeps the one-WP rule (another WP's tests dir fails
rule 1). Tests-only and tests-correction PRs stay available; they are no
longer required (FEATURE rule).

FEATURE rule (owner 2026-11-23, plan rev6.17: one PR per feature; tests +
code together; PATHS ride along). It replaces the rev6.3 (b) BATCH
IMPLEMENTATION rule (at most 3 WPs, disjoint PATHS, no in-batch Depends) and
the COUPLED rule (scripts/laned/coupled_batches): neither cap nor refusal
applies any more. It holds for a diff with code (a path outside
tests/accept/) and either a tests/accept/ path or more than one label:
    labels: laned:<WP> only (non-wp-label), no two naming the same dir
        (duplicate-label); laned:tests-correction is a flag, never a WP;
    tests: every tests/accept/ path is inside a labelled WP's dir
        (stray-tests, unlabelled-tests); every WP has PATHS and a test file
        at HEAD, on the base or added by this PR (no-tests);
    PATHS ride along: a WP's scope is its BASE PATHS plus the entries this PR
        adds (HEAD); an entry the PR removes still counts from BASE (a
        removal never takes a changed file out of scope). `PATHS-ADDED` /
        `PATHS-REMOVED <WP> <entry>` lines list them. An added entry matching
        nearly everything (empty, `*`, starting with a glob) or inside another
        WP's tests dir is refused (`FAIL paths: batch-paths-too-broad` /
        `batch-paths-foreign-tests`);
    scope: every changed code file is in some labelled WP's scope (a file in
        several is owned by each); none, a PROTECTED file, or a scripts/laned/
        file outside W0c's -> `FAIL paths: batch-out-of-scope`; every label
        owns a changed file or test (label-without-changes);
    weakening (anti-gaming): for each WP whose tests dir is in the diff,
        check-pr compares BASE and HEAD: a deleted test (or asserting
        helper) module, a removed test or asserting function, fewer
        assertions (`assert`, pytest.raises/warns/fail, *.assert_*) in a
        function or helper module, more skip/skipif/xfail/importorskip
        markers, or a removed PATHS entry -> `weakened <WP>: ...` lines and
        `FAIL weakened`, unless the PR also carries laned:tests-correction
        (--correction), when they are printed as allowed.
When it holds check-pr prints `BATCH <WPs>` (more than one label),
`SCOPE <WP> <n> file(s)` per WP, `WPMODE <WP> combined|implementation` per WP
(more than one label) and `MODE combined` (the WP's tests dir is in the diff)
or `MODE implementation` for the WP it was asked about. The PR job runs each
WP's own mode (--combined or the full Accept), one ledger row per WP (with
the PR number) and CROSS with the whole set as the PR's own WPs.
LABELLED L2 rule (status board, built_on, CROSS; lanedlib.impl_commit_index):
a squash merge `... (#N)` whose PR labels are known is a WP's L2 commit iff
PR N carried laned:<WP> AND the merge changes at least one non-test path
(outside tests/) the WP's PATHS match. A known PR without the label is never
the WP's L2, however its files sit in the WP's PATHS (#817 made W5i read L2
through broad PATHS). Labels come from $LANED_PR_LABELS (the workflow writes
`gh pr list --state merged --json number,labels`; the step fails closed) and
the ledger's PR rows (one per laned:<WP> label, field `pr`; laned/build-state
or $LANED_LEDGER). A green `combined` row also makes the merge that changes
tests/accept/<wp>/ the WP's L1 (lanedlib.pr_merge_index). Only a commit
with no PR number or unknown labels falls back to the git-only rules: a
single-WP implementation commit, a rev6.3 batch commit
(lanedlib.is_batch_impl_commit) and a declared coupled-set commit
(lanedlib.is_coupled_impl_commit; coupled_batches is still read for that,
nothing new is declared there).

CROSS rule (the PR job runs it after the PR's own WP steps, every mode but
review): a WP other than the PR's own WP(s) is re-accepted when
    (a) it is built on BASE: tests/accept/<wp>/PATHS exists at BASE and either
        BASE's first-parent line has its L2 commit (lanedlib.impl_commit_index,
        the board's L2 rule), or it is a verification WP (listed in
        scripts/laned/verification_wps at BASE) whose non-test PATHS all exist
        at BASE (the board's L2 via verification; without the ledger its green
        verification run is not re-checked, nor is the board's L1 ledger
        precondition, so a WP in doubt is included), and
    (b) its tests depend on `git diff --name-only BASE...HEAD`: the diff touches
        a global input (pyproject.toml, uv.lock, tests/conftest.py, ...), its
        own test dir, a path its PATHS match, a repo module in the static
        import closure of its test dir (AST, transitive through repo modules
        and package __init__.py; absolute imports resolve from the repo root,
        the importing file's dir, tests/accept and tests), or a path named by a
        string constant in its test files ("cascade/x/y.json", "chain.toml").
The tests-correction / tests-only / verification WP X of the PR is its own
main step, so X is never a cross WP. A cross WP runs invariant 10, pytest over
its test dir (all cross WPs in one pytest run, split only where two
non-package test dirs share a module basename) and stub_scan over its PATHS;
no e2e (the main step ran it). A failure the junit report cannot attribute to
one WP fails every WP of that run.
A cross WP whose pytest fails on HEAD is re-run at BASE (a temporary detached
worktree of BASE, same LANED_PYTEST_CMD, removed afterwards) and compared per
junit test id:
    CROSS <WP> KNOWN  every test failing on HEAD also fails on BASE (already
                      red on main; printed with the ids; does not fail the job)
    CROSS <WP> FAIL   a test that passes on BASE fails on HEAD (a regression),
                      or either run is not plain test failures (no report,
                      unattributed failures, a collection error, nothing
                      collected): fails closed.

FRESHNESS rule (the PR job's last step, RV5): BASE is the base the run tested
(the merge commit's first parent), MAIN is origin/main fetched at job time.
BASE == MAIN is fresh. Otherwise, with MB = merge-base(BASE, MAIN), the PR is
STALE when its diff (BASE...HEAD) and main's diff (MB..MAIN) interact, in the
PR's tree or in MAIN's: a path both touch; a changed module of one side whose
static import closure reaches a changed path of the other; or a WP test dir
that depends (CROSS rule (b)) on both diffs. Unrelated main commits leave the
PR fresh. Since the GitHub merge queue (ruleset 27870327, 2026-11-23) the
pull_request job runs it with --warn-only: a stale PR gets a WARNING in the
job summary, not a failure, so a green PR no longer chases main through
update-branch / re-run loops. The gate moved to the queue (MERGE-GROUP rule).

MERGE-GROUP rule (the merge_group run, owner 2026-11-24): HEAD is the queue's
combined ref (its working tree at --root), BASE is merge_group.base_sha. Each
first-parent commit C of BASE..HEAD is one queued PR (`... (#N)` squash or
`Merge pull request #N ...`); --group-labels maps N to the PR's labels. For
every laned:<WP> of N, `--check-pr --base C^1 --head C` names the mode and the
WP's own run follows ON THE COMBINED TREE: implementation and combined -> the
full Accept (the combined mode's base-failing half ran on the PR); tests-only
-> --verification; tests-correction -> --tests-correction; evidence -> the full
Accept with each EVIDENCE file; review -> nothing. A WP several queued PRs
carry runs once. A commit naming no PR, a PR missing from the labels file, a
check-pr failure or any failing run fails the group (closed). The CROSS rule
then runs over BASE..HEAD excluding the `GROUP-OWN` WPs (their own Accept just
ran). Nothing is ledger-recorded (the push job records after the merge).

The pytest command is `uv run --frozen pytest` unless LANED_PYTEST_CMD is set
(shlex-split). It always runs with cwd = --root. Exit 0 means pass.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
